WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.100
Hi, everyone. Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners Episode 91 for July 3, 2026.

00:00:06.980 --> 00:00:10.780
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:15.600
Some awesome AI developments today, and we're going to make all of it make sense.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:20.680
Let's get into it. Here's something that's going to change how creative projects feel for people

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:26.700
who have never touched code before. Meta released a new experimental app called Pocket that

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:31.180
turns simple text descriptions into small playable games you can share right away.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:35.720
Instead of learning complicated design programs or programming languages,

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.480
you describe what you want and the system builds it on the spot.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:45.960
Picture this like telling a story to a very fast friend who can instantly turn your words into a toy

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:51.520
you can hold and play with on your phone. The games stay short and light, the kind of thing you

00:00:51.520 --> 00:00:57.160
might finish in a minute or two, such as a quick puzzle about matching colors or a silly race with

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:03.180
cartoon characters. This matters because making games used to require months of practice and expensive

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:10.500
software, but now the barrier drops to just typing an idea. Students who enjoy roblox or mobile games

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:14.840
can suddenly shift from playing to creating their own simple versions in seconds.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:21.080
One clear personal takeaway is that AI is starting to move beyond answering questions and

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:27.080
into building things you can interact with immediately. You can search for Meta Pocket in your

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:32.860
app store or on the web right now to check availability in your area and try prompts like a simple

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:38.700
endless runner with a cat character. The app is still experimental, so what you see today might

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:45.040
look different next week. Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the deep dive where we look under

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:51.160
the hood. Today's concept is AI agents, which are helpers that take a goal and carry it out over

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:57.080
multiple steps instead of stopping after one answer. Think of it like ordering food through a delivery

00:01:57.080 --> 00:02:03.200
app, where the system does not just show a menu, but also finds a driver, tracks the order, and updates

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:10.060
you if anything changes along the way. An AI agent works the same way for digital tasks, breaking your

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:16.120
request into smaller pieces, using different tools as needed, and keeping track of progress until the

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:21.960
whole job finishes. For example, if you asked an agent to research and write a short report on your

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:28.280
favorite band, it might first look up recent news, then gather facts from several sites, then organize

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:34.420
the information into paragraphs, and finally review its own work for errors. The agent remembers

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:39.600
what it has already completed and what still needs attention. Much like the delivery app keeps

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:46.000
updating your order status in real time. What sets this apart from a regular chatbot is the ability

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.680
to act across time and switch between skills rather than answering one question and then stopping.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:59.480
Recent measurements show these agents now finish about 16% of real freelance tasks at a professional

00:02:59.480 --> 00:03:06.880
level up from just 2.5% only 8 months ago. The improvement comes from giving the AI clearer ways

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:13.240
to plan and check its own steps instead of guessing everything at once. When you hear the term AI

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:18.760
agents, picture a helpful assistant that can carry out an entire project instead of just chatting

00:03:18.760 --> 00:03:24.980
for a moment. That view makes the progress feel less mysterious and more like a natural next step

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:30.820
in how tools help us get things done. Let's move into some cool stuff you can actually try today,

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:35.980
starting with better creative writing tools. Today's models are getting stronger at

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:42.480
open-ended tasks like storytelling or brainstorming, even when there is no single right answer to check

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:48.640
against. This matters because it means AI can now help with school projects that involve writing stories

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:55.580
or coming up with fresh ideas for art assignments. Go to any major chatbot site, type a prompt such as

00:03:55.580 --> 00:04:01.740
continue this short story about a robot discovering music, and notice how the output feels more natural

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:07.360
and less repetitive than earlier versions. You can experiment with different story lengths or

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:13.980
styles to see where the improvements show up most clearly. Next up is a new set of AI tools inside

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:20.380
the design platform Figma that handle motion and visual effects. A recent tutorial shows how to

00:04:20.380 --> 00:04:26.720
combine Figma's built-in AI agent with manual controls to create custom animations and filters without

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:33.100
building everything from scratch. If you already use Figma for school projects or personal design work,

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:39.680
this opens the door to adding movement to your graphics in just a few clicks. Open Figma in your browser,

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:45.780
start a new file, and try asking the AI agent to generate a simple bouncing animation for a circle

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:51.760
shape. Then tweak the speed yourself to see how the controls work together. The whole process stays

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:58.200
free for basic use and requires no coding knowledge. Time for quick bits, starting with creative writing

00:04:58.200 --> 00:05:04.620
gains. Researchers and creators notice that current models handle storytelling and design tasks better

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:10.580
even without clear right or wrong answers. The progress is happening faster than some experts

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:17.700
expected according to posts on X. This improvement allows AI to assist with open-ended creative work,

00:05:17.700 --> 00:05:23.920
such as writing stories or brainstorming ideas for school projects. One post from researcher

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:29.480
Ethan Mollick highlights how models now manage tasks like these more effectively than before.

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:35.920
The change means students and hobbyists can use AI for assignments that lack a single correct answer.

